+### 12.1 REMOTE v1 operational persistence threat model
+
+REMOTE v1 MUST preserve crash consistency and detect detectable corruption under ordinary power-loss at any point of writing, torn/incomplete writes, interrupted NVS operations and inconsistent or stale roots versus records. If the newest committed state cannot be determined from the recovered persistent state, recovery MUST fail closed for every affected scope; a valid older root or individually valid records alone MUST NOT justify accepting an older state. Recovery MUST emit zero commands. Tests MUST use an independent dispatch/emission witness which is not reconstructed from, or rolled back with, the tested persistent domain.
+
+G1a (REQUIRED) denotes newest-state consistency after ordinary power-loss and partial persistent-domain operations. Request/effect/barrier correlation, prevention of command replay after ordinary power-loss, allocator non-reuse in the accepted recovered history, and all mandatory unresolved barriers, evidence and lifetime non-reuse metadata MUST remain intact. Insufficient capacity MUST cause refusal before emission; mandatory data MUST NOT be truncated, evicted or silently omitted. Migration MUST be explicit; unknown schemas and ambiguous migration MUST fail closed without emission. Other normative requirements remain unchanged.
+
+G1b (OUTSIDE V1 THREAT MODEL) denotes anti-rollback against intentional restoration of the entire NVS partition, cloning an older internally valid flash image, adversarial simultaneous rollback of the root and all records, or an attacker capable of rewriting the entire persistent flash domain. REMOTE v1 does not guarantee detection or semantic survival of those complete coherent restorations. Such cases MUST be reported as OUTSIDE V1 THREAT MODEL, never as a PASS furnished by NVS. This exclusion MUST NOT be applied to partial restoration, stale/inconsistent root versus retained records, ordinary interrupted writes, logical association rollback, administrative deletion/reset, unknown schema, or fencing/reconciliation requirements.
+
+This REMOTE-specific threat boundary MUST NOT require TUNER to be a monotonic authority, acquire mandatory persistent storage, remain continuously available, implement Native API, or change its existing firmware. Future reconciliation MUST NOT automatically reexecute commands. Ordered append records, root/generation commits and recovery validation remain candidate mechanisms requiring separate implementation and physical qualification, not guarantees established by this specification.
 
